#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180559} :activity/id #uuid "c9655728-f410-56c7-8943-6d053246e392" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049041 :topic/id #uuid "d53c1458-9af5-589b-8d7b-d3dbe4e7a946" :topic/math-academy-id 3706} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180559 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180760 :tutorial/content "To find the image of a point under an affine transformation, all we need to do is substitute the position vector of the point into the formula for the affine transformation.\n\nFor example, consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\Bbb R^2 \\to \\Bbb R^2,$ defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSuppose that the point $P({\\color{black}-1},{\\color{black}1})$ with position vector $\\mathbf p$ lies in the $uv$-plane, as shown below. \n\n![](images/06/06c9283bde54e329d31d5892702e4a75c0ca78bd21ee3bb23f9a2b748239bf14.png)\n\nWe can find the image of $P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by substituting $\\mathbf p$ into the affine transformation formula: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(P) & =Ap+b \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of $P$ is the point $P'({\\color{black}-1},{\\color{black}3})$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nWe can visualize $P$ and its image under $\\mathbf T$ using a diagram like the one below.\n\n![](images/89/893933990225c54e0a3327de70f29b3d73af2b829cf53053d155bb52d5202e63.png)\n\nWe can use a similar method to map multiple points from the $uv$-plane to the $xy$-plane using just one calculation. Let's see how." :tutorial/id #uuid "b71d5e38-7267-5428-89f5-941492316127" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7694 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7e42cdff-0162-5f00-9062-0bbf4e7ef4c8" :step/math-academy-id 44648 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180560}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180560 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179773 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179434 :question/id #uuid "102d79a9-f19c-5735-a3f2-347938820b19" :question/math-academy-id "e-12425"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ba09dd1-d219-5df2-adfa-d88d7b9fc481" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049426}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259604} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259605} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259606} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259613} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371699} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387469}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Point or Segment"} :step/id #uuid "130575e3-c9fd-5688-a557-9be7454f3b61" :step/math-academy-id 44649 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180561}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180561 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180761 :tutorial/content "Consider the triangle $\\Delta$ with sides $S_1, S_2,$ and $S_3,$ shown below. Let's imagine that we traverse the boundary once in the counterclockwise direction starting from the origin.\n\n![](images/23/231dd5f96cd36d21940fcee894382edc9eabc91b0bf72bb4a8724c8444f76a8c.png)\n\nLet the affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ be defined as\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & ={{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTo find the image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing all vertices of our triangle, presented in the order shown in the diagram: $$U = [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$$\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $\\mathbf b$ in each column: $$B = [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nThe image of $\\mathbf u$ under $T$ is given by $A\\mathbf u + \\mathbf b.$ So, by applying $\\mathbf T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe vertices of the image are given by the columns of the above matrix. Drawing these vertices in the order shown, i.e.\n\n$$(1,-1) \\to (1,0) \\to (2,-1),$$\n\nwe get the following picture:\n\n![](images/d5/d514834e6e9f108f478002416e863cf9c98d00add230c2e9321664ea14e5db0e.png)\n\nNotice that the polygon $D$ is traversed in the *clockwise* direction." :tutorial/id #uuid "c717442d-1c85-597b-bfc9-3bacde35a1f0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7695 :tutorial/title "Finding the Image of a Polygon"} :step/id #uuid "804e3ae7-0f27-5ff7-9101-bf7c7b3dfa01" :step/math-academy-id 44652 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180562}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180562 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180762 :tutorial/content "An invertible affine transformation is **orientation-preserving** if it preserves the orientation of any polygon in the plane.\n\nTo understand this, let's go back to our triangle $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane and its image $D$ in the $xy$-plane under the action of the affine transformation $\\mathbf T.$\n\n![](images/b5/b5c33feb218ae0c1c32aa61984d01b5a317ab88f78277e01c20111a2c0c4efe5.png)\n\nThis transformation does *not* preserve orientation because when we traverse $\\Delta$ *counterclockwise*, its image $D$ is traversed *clockwise.*\n\nWe can identify whether an affine transformation is orientation-preserving or not by examining the matrix $A$ of the associated linear map. We have the following theorem:\n\n*Suppose an affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is defined as* $$\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,$$ *where $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix. Then, $\\mathbf{T}$ preserves orientation if* $$\\det(A) > 0,$$ *and does not preserve orientation if* $$\\det(A) < 0.$$\n\nWe sometimes refer to cases where $\\det(A) < 0$ as **orientation-reversing.**" :tutorial/id #uuid "0444feba-dbdb-5165-942a-79da29a810e0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7696 :tutorial/title "Orientation-Preserving Transformations"} :step/id #uuid "3f2a959e-2593-532e-916d-db411d030416" :step/math-academy-id 44653 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180563}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180563 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179774 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179435 :question/id #uuid "290ea83a-1a47-5260-82b2-346d38e9adcb" :question/math-academy-id "e-12426"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fd0b67f-681b-5b1d-8be9-2ab2d1ca5e66" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259698} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387532}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Polygon Under an Affine Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "bfcdd1b6-82a4-5529-8d93-b859895ed88c" :step/math-academy-id 44654 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180564}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180564 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179775 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179436 :question/id #uuid "0f180b16-c807-555d-b92c-826a216a31e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-12427"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36996ffc-7dbc-51f8-8efe-46ed2c897045" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049050}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259669} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259674} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371706}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Affine Transformation is Orientation-Preserving"} :step/id #uuid "db19ad89-86be-5bd4-8aba-82b5b0c23c42" :step/math-academy-id 44657 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180573}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180570 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179779 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179440 :question/id #uuid "e6f17219-642b-51d3-bebe-61519ee6651f" :question/math-academy-id "e-12436"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1577f765-f98c-5020-8e45-8da2c5f092e6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049345}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372013}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Area Scale Factor of an Affine Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "6e8df5a8-690a-5bd7-acaa-c40815a5fca6" :step/math-academy-id 44685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180573 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180766 :tutorial/content "Suppose an affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is defined as $$\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,$$ where $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nSimilar to the case of linear transformations, the **area scale factor** of $\\mathbf T$ is given by the determinant of $A.$\n\nWe can identify whether an affine transformation increases or decreases the area of a polygon $\\mathcal P$ by examining the area scale factor:\n\n- If $|\\det(A)|>1,$ the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ will have a *larger* area than $\\mathcal P.$\n- If $|\\det(A)|=1,$ the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ will have the *same* area as $\\mathcal P.$\n- If $0 < |\\det(A)| < 1,$ the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ will have a *smaller* area than $\\mathcal P.$\n\nNote that if $\\det(A) = 0,$ the image of $\\mathcal P$ is not a polygon. We discuss this case in more detail in a separate lesson.\n\nFor example, consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2,$ from the previous discussion, defined as\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & ={{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get $$\\det(A) = \\left| \\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned} \\right| = 0 \\cdot 0 - 1 \\cdot 1 =-1.$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is $$|\\det(A)| = |-1| = 1.$$\n\nThus, the area of triangle $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane and its image $D$ under $\\mathbf T$ in the $xy$-plane have the same area.\n\n![](images/b5/b5c33feb218ae0c1c32aa61984d01b5a317ab88f78277e01c20111a2c0c4efe5.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "e306ae2c-f307-52ed-a9fc-9376bdfeaa2e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7705 :tutorial/title "The Area Scale Factor of an Affine Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "0c8f36a3-dfdb-59b6-82cb-0413df0361cd" :step/math-academy-id 44703 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180570}}] :activity/title "The Image of an Affine Transformation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179865}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046665} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408074} :activity/id #uuid "eb78a48d-c31f-5955-bc9e-cdc4e0762d1c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049041 :topic/id #uuid "d53c1458-9af5-589b-8d7b-d3dbe4e7a946" :topic/math-academy-id 3706} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408073 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259668 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cbf8e90-0fcd-5e9d-ad8e-5d029388c5ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257331} {:answer/id #uuid "43d247fa-abed-5958-b466-10fc02c6fd8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257385} {:answer/id #uuid "4681c1f9-2a9f-5136-9cf7-ed7506a00643" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257404} {:answer/id #uuid "5d0eefab-460e-5b30-b83c-d3d6a7dee230" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257599} {:answer/id #uuid "7277e3e9-81db-502b-97dc-3210914ad7a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257794}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257331} :answer-field/id #uuid "ced03044-c848-5e8d-a236-e8367ae6dd32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d6b75fa0-1178-5417-bc31-861bbdef5229" :question/math-academy-id "q-124321" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=2v-u+2,\\,y=2u+v-5.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n- $det(A)=-5$\n- The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2v-u+2 \\\\ 2u+v-5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-u+2v \\\\ 2u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|=(-1)\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 2=-5.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $det(A)<0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is not orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "0a78ca60-8278-5f69-a0c5-4853a7109868" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408075}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408074 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259622 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ccab3c0-0254-5090-9c86-829a851b15b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256913} {:answer/id #uuid "2c4c2cb2-f173-5288-817b-371883d27529" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257185} {:answer/id #uuid "60740839-3d41-5df6-8849-4084e44e6ec1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257636} {:answer/id #uuid "b74a87c3-7a0f-5446-af5b-58a1007116ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258388} {:answer/id #uuid "d661a75a-735f-5ece-a15b-6a8a834246d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258671}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258388} :answer-field/id #uuid "33b4cd81-c3a3-52e0-a0d4-355e8ea3aed5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d0a1ba57-9eac-5d49-9cdd-f7f25fec8c92" :question/math-academy-id "q-124192" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=2v+u+1,\\,y=u-v-2.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\n- $det(A)=-3$\n- The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2v+u+1 \\\\ u-v-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u+2v \\\\ u-v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|=1\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 1=-3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $det(A)<0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is not orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "88a789c1-e3c2-5586-9aab-92005c1e4bcd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408073}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408075 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259696 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75895875-a73e-5cbb-bf3d-c5aa2a0d474a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "images/d1/d1e93d9ce18d7c75ab6432d53ae9ff72fd3fe2c89125a12dcd2d41a13100465d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257819} {:answer/id #uuid "7e96b286-9f53-5a16-a78e-11d14b7729bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "images/82/8254c51a8f1c9f0ca48f169fb48a938b07e5812b5be866d9ab25e5fbdffed368.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257914} {:answer/id #uuid "8db86e0e-3c7f-5fc7-8dd6-cb016f66e98b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "images/6b/6be30b91e0526e28530b4c5cfff6c0c5e7be53a77e587c5eb37a845b6cc0a189.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258034} {:answer/id #uuid "aac5b30f-88de-5a6d-a659-c1f1d57058a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "images/a7/a7b171394aeccd9b807fc29ecf7c33f745c58bce3ed01bcf879a79d2edf97319.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258277} {:answer/id #uuid "c68d0b11-70cb-55fa-8c98-86952dd0839e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "images/27/27dbdbec3125bdef5109bf6aa549c9bcfe83175b42e660cc44977540de0ad112.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258523}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257819} :answer-field/id #uuid "047ad574-9503-5413-9fac-632c989f1e97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6fdc3f93-8486-56f6-8744-50fa49ca621b" :question/math-academy-id "q-124561" :question/problem "![](images/79/79b09bcf02998adf6d7bf19e7d59cabdc563ff26266966440bbb49be53029ac3.png)\n\nConsider the triangle $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,-\\frac{u+1}{2}\\le v\\le u+1}.\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the image of $Δ$ in the $xy$-plane under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation is already in matrix form \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & ={{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ maps the boundary of $Δ$ to the boundary of the image. So, to find the image of $Δ,$ we find the images of each side.\n\n![](images/53/539c74bf4d397916fd7ac11089fa9500bd71cdf2278891136785d6e792431b05.png)\n\n             \n\nTo quickly carry out this calculation, we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing all vertices of our triangle:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $b$ in each column:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe image of $u$ under $T$ is given by $Au+b.$ So, by applying $T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertices of the image in the $xy$-plane are $(-1,-1),(1,-1),$ and $(-2,2).$\n\nDrawing this region in the $xy$-plane, we get the following:\n\n![](images/ad/adfe476bd2ca719996d612855ca5368bdea09370b7befcffb08a062db8587cbb.png)\n\n          \n\nFinally, since $det(A)=-1<0,$ this transformation is not orientation-preserving."} :step/id #uuid "de3c23cf-80a3-5af4-81aa-d8176ded4ab6"}] :activity/title "The Image of an Affine Transformation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408072}]}
